{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f7e050751d7d4419be8050cd9202647f","ordinal":321,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.80","text":"\n\nEven if we were to grant, by hypothesis, that there is some criterion of truth, it turns out to be useless and pointless once we point out that, as far as what the dogmatists say goes, truth is unreal and what is true has no real existence."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c9000c6f54b04a559e1fa77150e634b9","ordinal":322,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.81","text":"\n\nWe point this out as follows. What is true is said to differ from truth in three ways: in essence, in composition and in power. In essence, because what is true is incorporeal (for it is a proposition and a sayable), whereas truth is a body. For truth is knowledge capable of asserting everything true, and knowledge is the ruling part in a certain state, just as a fist is the hand in a certain state; and the ruling part is a body, since according to them it is breath.\n〔1〕 Stoic term: a “sayable” (lekton) is the incorporeal content of what is said."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-48696fab5fd84f6ba5c22323b29c03fb","ordinal":323,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.82","text":"\n\nIn composition, because what is true is something simple, like “I am conversing,” whereas truth is made up of many pieces of true knowledge."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-929384551441426b83e9bc635f2a5bb9","ordinal":324,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.83","text":"\n\nIn power, because truth involves knowledge, whereas what is true does not always do so. That is why they say that truth is found only in the good person, while what is true is found in the bad person too, since a bad person can say something true. So much for the dogmatists."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b4d4adae793a4a5d9da1775d90622644","ordinal":325,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.84","text":"\n\nFor our part, we will keep in view once again the aim of this treatise and direct our arguments for now against what is true alone. Truth, which is said to be a system of knowledge of true things, is cancelled along with it. Again, some of our arguments are more general: by these we challenge the very existence of what is true. Others are specific: by these we show that what is true is not in the utterance, or in the sayable, or in the motion of the intellect. We think it enough for the present to set out only the more general ones. When the foundation of a wall is brought down, everything built on top comes down with it. In the same way, when the existence of what is true is overturned, the dogmatists’ ingenious arguments on particular points are cancelled along with it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6b7f15fb266f4a13b14668b48e4edaaf","ordinal":326,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.85","text":"\n\nThere is disagreement among the dogmatists about what is true: some say that something is true, others that nothing is. This disagreement cannot be decided. Whoever says that something is true will not be believed if he says it without proof, because of the disagreement. And if he wants to offer a proof as well, he will not be believed if he admits that the proof is false. If he says that the proof is true, he falls into the mode of reciprocity, and he will be asked for a proof ⟨of⟩ its being true, then another for that one, and so on to infinity. But it is impossible to prove infinitely many things. So it is impossible even to know that something is true."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f0e0bfa90977424c82d2f5ae216117c0","ordinal":327,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.86","text":"\n\nAgain, the “something,” which they say is the most generic of all things, is either true or false, or neither false nor true, or both false and true. Now if they say it is false, they will be admitting that everything is false. Just as, since animal has a soul, all particular animals have souls too, so if the most generic of all things (the “something”) is false, all the particulars will be false too, and nothing will be true. This brings with it the consequence that nothing is false either. For “everything is false” and “something is false” are themselves among all things, so they too will be false. If the “something” is true, everything will be true. This brings with it, again, the consequence that nothing is true, since this very thing (I mean “nothing is true”) is a something, and so it is true."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e47a932149674888b1811b744e75f097","ordinal":328,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.87","text":"\n\nIf the “something” is both false and true, each particular will be both false and true. It follows that nothing is true by nature, since what has a nature such as to be true would certainly not be false. And if the “something” is neither false nor true, it is admitted that all the particulars too, said to be neither false nor true, will not be true. For these reasons, then, it will be non-evident to us whether there is anything true."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-696f1ea1a319456181a111b1c438a209","ordinal":329,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.88","text":"\n\nFurther, true things are either only apparent, or only non-evident, or some true things are non-evident and some apparent. None of these is true, as we will show; so nothing is true. Now if true things are only apparent, they will say either that all apparent things are true or that some are. If all, the argument turns itself around, since it appears to some people that nothing is true. If some, no one can say without deciding the matter that these are true and those false. And if he uses a criterion, he will say that this criterion is either apparent or non-evident. It cannot possibly be non-evident, since the hypothesis now is that only apparent things are true."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-65101f036450435787385816304edf54","ordinal":330,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.89","text":"\n\nIf it is apparent, then, since the question is which apparent things are true and which false, the apparent thing adopted to judge apparent things will itself need another apparent criterion, and that one another, and so on to infinity. But it is impossible to decide infinitely many things. So it is impossible to apprehend whether only apparent things are true."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f4d7d97d7b284becbb37e9cd345b2cdd","ordinal":331,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.90","text":"\n\nSimilarly, someone who says that only non-evident things are true will not say that all of them are true; he will not say that the stars’ being even in number is true and also their being odd. And if some, by what will we judge that these non-evident things are true and those false? Certainly not by something apparent. If by something non-evident, then, since we are asking which non-evident things are true and which false, this non-evident thing too will need another non-evident thing to decide it, and that one another, and so on to infinity. So true things are not only non-evident either."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1cc1a4826d794dc294ab0be2c031143d","ordinal":332,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.91","text":"\n\nIt remains to say that some true things are apparent and some non-evident. But this too is absurd. Either all apparent and non-evident things are true, or some apparent ones and some non-evident ones. If all, the argument will again turn itself around, since “nothing is true” is granted to be true. And both “the stars are even in number” and “they are odd” will be called true."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0899106a4df14d3e9ec69ba4785b8e77","ordinal":333,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.92","text":"\n\nIf some apparent and some non-evident things are true, how will we decide that, among apparent things, these are true and those false? ⟨For⟩ if by something apparent, the argument is thrown into infinite regress. If by something non-evident, then, since non-evident things too need to be judged, by what will this non-evident thing be judged in turn? ⟨For⟩ if by something apparent, the mode of reciprocity turns up; if by something non-evident, the mode that throws us into infinite regress."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a17c9dddf0274a308e3e1ae878300ccd","ordinal":334,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.93","text":"\n\nThe same must be said about non-evident things. Someone who tries to judge them by something non-evident is thrown into infinite regress. Someone who judges them by something apparent either keeps adding something apparent and goes on to infinity, or switches to something non-evident and falls into reciprocity. So it is false to say that some true things are apparent and some non-evident."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-de716517adc441eba505d9beae75ddd0","ordinal":335,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.94","text":"\n\nIf, then, true things are not apparent things alone, nor non-evident things alone, nor some apparent and some non-evident, nothing is true. And if nothing is true, while the criterion is thought to serve for judging what is true, the criterion is useless and pointless, even if we grant, for the sake of argument, that it has some existence. And if we must suspend judgment about whether anything is true, it follows that those who say dialectic is the knowledge of what is true, what is false and what is neither are being rash."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b9cba4bf71d24d8fa112e985bf4d209b","ordinal":336,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.95","text":"\n\nNow that the criterion of truth has turned out to be problematic, it is no longer possible, as far as what the dogmatists say goes, to make firm assertions either about things that seem to be clear or about non-evident things. The dogmatists think they apprehend non-evident things on the basis of clear ones. So if we are forced to suspend judgment about the so-called clear things, how could we dare to make declarations about non-evident ones?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d9964b1e4f5748288487f8097c1bc4fa","ordinal":337,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.96","text":"\n\nStill, for good measure, we will also mount a separate challenge against non-evident things. Since these are thought to be apprehended and established by means of sign and proof, we will point out briefly that it is proper to suspend judgment about sign and proof as well. Let us begin with the sign, since proof too is thought to be, generically, a sign."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-edb611d92b404a5ea7625a313a423ab4","ordinal":338,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.97","text":"\n\nAccording to the dogmatists, some things are evident and others non-evident. Of the non-evident, some are non-evident once and for all, some non-evident for the moment, and some non-evident by nature. Evident, they say, are the things that come to our knowledge of themselves, such as its being day. Non-evident once and for all are the things that are not of a nature to fall within our apprehension, such as the stars’ being even in number."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-91c982c790ab42f1b3eb56bff2fa628d","ordinal":339,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.98","text":"\n\nNon-evident for the moment are the things that are clear by nature but are made non-evident to us for the time being by certain external circumstances, as the city of Athens is now to me. Non-evident by nature are the things that are not of a nature to fall within our clear perception, like the intelligible pores. These never appear of themselves; if at all, they would be thought to be apprehended from other things, such as sweat or something of that sort.\n〔1〕 “Intelligible pores” were invisible channels in the skin posited by some doctors and philosophers, detectable only by reasoning (for instance, from sweating)."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4d1390a11f2b48818c015b92d6c094ed","ordinal":340,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.99","text":"\n\nEvident things, they say, do not need a sign, since they are apprehended of themselves. Nor do things that are non-evident once and for all, since they are not apprehended at all. Things non-evident for the moment and things non-evident by nature are apprehended through signs, but not through the same ones: things non-evident for the moment through recollective signs, and things non-evident by nature through indicative ones."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c681d32b01434ddf97bf302080eb5bd4","ordinal":341,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.100","text":"\n\nOf signs, then, according to them, some are recollective and some indicative. They call a sign recollective when it has been clearly observed together with the thing it signifies. When it strikes us while that thing is non-evident, it leads us to recall what was observed together with it and is not now clearly perceived. This is how it is with smoke and fire."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3b574291d0814c67bea58f48b381409b","ordinal":342,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.101","text":"\n\nAn indicative sign, they say, is one that has not been clearly observed together with the thing signified. It signifies what it is a sign of from its own nature and constitution, as the movements of the body are signs of the soul. Hence they define this sign as follows: “A sign is an indicative proposition that is the leading antecedent in a sound conditional and reveals the consequent.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-50dc97fc8e564479944d0c60e0171720","ordinal":343,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.102","text":"\n\nThere are two different kinds of signs, then, as we said. We do not argue against every sign but only against the indicative one, since it seems to have been invented by the dogmatists. The recollective sign is relied on by ordinary life: someone who sees smoke takes it as a sign of fire, and on seeing a scar says there has been a wound. So far from fighting against ordinary life, we even take its side. We assent, without holding beliefs, to what it relies on, while we resist what the dogmatists invent on their own account."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7ae3ce3e2e84452491fd382c58f5860d","ordinal":344,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.103","text":"\n\nIt was perhaps fitting to say this first, to make the subject of inquiry clear. Now let us move on to the counterargument. We are not at all eager to show that the indicative sign is unreal. We aim to point out the apparent equipollence of the arguments brought for its reality and for its unreality."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fa65e2c215774fe982f31e4c0aedb45a","ordinal":345,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.104","text":"\n\nAs far as what the dogmatists say about it goes, the sign is inconceivable. To start with, the Stoics, who seem to have defined it precisely, want to set out the concept of the sign and say that a sign is a proposition that is the leading antecedent in a sound conditional and reveals the consequent. A proposition, they say, is a complete sayable that is assertoric in itself. A sound conditional is one that does not begin with something true and end with something false."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f637f7cec8fe43fd88396ec7bfe263b9","ordinal":346,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.105","text":"\n\nFor a conditional either begins with something true and ends with something true, like “if it is day, it is light.” Or it begins with something false and ends with something false, like “if the earth flies, the earth has wings.” Or it begins with something true and ends with something false, like “if the earth exists, the earth flies.” Or it begins with something false and ends with something true, like “if the earth flies, the earth exists.” Of these, they say only the one that begins with something true and ends with something false is unsound, and the rest are sound."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-a6c74518cd4a424d92c6d5cd754788fc","ordinal":347,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.106","text":"\n\nA leading antecedent, they say, is the antecedent in a conditional that begins with something true and ends with something true. And it reveals the consequent, since in the conditional “if she has milk, she has conceived,” “she has milk” is thought to make clear “she has conceived.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-3c707cfb5d6e45879e7c667fb8d6f129","ordinal":348,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.107","text":"\n\nSo much for them. We say, first, that it is non-evident whether there is ⟨any⟩ sayable. Among the dogmatists, the Epicureans say that there is not ⟨any⟩ sayable and the Stoics that there is. So when the Stoics say that there is a sayable, they use either mere assertion or proof as well. If assertion, the Epicureans will set against them the assertion that there is no sayable. If they bring in a proof, then, since proof is composed of propositions, which are sayables, a proof composed of sayables cannot be adopted to confirm that there is a sayable (for how will someone who does not grant that there is a sayable concede that a system of sayables exists?)"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0b37901d4e1140b78bc382afa6a91f63","ordinal":349,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.108","text":"\n\n—so whoever tries to establish that there is a sayable from the existence of a system of sayables wants to confirm what is in question through what is in question. If, then, it is possible to establish that there is a sayable neither simply nor by proof, it is non-evident that there is a sayable. Likewise, it is non-evident whether there is a proposition,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d7aacc535bbe4197851b66ce5de25da7","ordinal":350,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.109","text":"\n\nsince a proposition is a sayable. Perhaps, too, even if it were granted by hypothesis that there is a sayable, the proposition would turn out to be unreal, since it is composed of sayables that do not exist together with one another. Take “if it is day, it is light.” When I say “it is day,” “it is light” does not yet exist, and when I say “it is light,” “it is day” no longer exists. Now things composed of parts cannot exist unless their parts exist together with one another, and the parts of which the proposition is composed do not exist together with one another. So the proposition will not exist."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0d4000a7b4984e599a3514c9bc0d7d6b","ordinal":351,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.110","text":"\n\nBut let us leave these points aside too: the sound conditional will turn out to be inapprehensible. Philo says that a sound conditional is one that does not begin with something true and end with something false. An example, when it is day and I am conversing, is “if it is day, I am conversing.” Diodorus says it is one that neither could nor can begin with something true and end with something false. According to him the conditional just mentioned seems to be false: when it is day and I have fallen silent, it will begin with something true and end with something false.\n〔1〕 Philo the Dialectician was a logician of the late fourth century BC, a pupil of Diodorus Cronus."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-1f0e1e2b459d48118581924a0b71bfa2","ordinal":352,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.111","text":"\n\nOn the other hand this one is true: “if there are no partless elements of things, there are partless elements of things.” For according to him it always begins with something false, “there are no partless elements of things,” and will end with something true, “there are partless elements of things.” Those who bring in connection say that a conditional is sound when the opposite of its consequent conflicts with its antecedent. According to them the conditionals just mentioned will be unsound, while this one is true: “if it is day, it is day.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ec58ee1ab5814344ab1b79e9141fee46","ordinal":353,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.112","text":"\n\nThose who judge by implication say that a conditional is true when its consequent is potentially contained in its antecedent. According to them “if it is day, it is day,” and every duplicated conditional [proposition], will perhaps be false,"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-eb7d1c299fa54107a13ac4491115e0e8","ordinal":354,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.113","text":"\n\nsince it is impossible for anything to be contained in itself. It will perhaps seem impossible, then, to decide this disagreement. We will not be credible if we prefer one of the positions just mentioned without proof, or with proof either. For a proof is thought to be sound when its conclusion follows from the conjunction of its premises as a consequent follows from an antecedent. For example: “If it is day, it is light; but it is day; therefore it is light”—“if it is day, it is light, and it is day, then it is light.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c25971e737f3405683ce723da45beed5","ordinal":355,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.114","text":"\n\nBut when the question is how we are to judge the following of the consequent from the antecedent, the mode of reciprocity turns up. For the judgment of the conditional to be proved, the conclusion must follow from the premises of the proof, as we said before. And for this in turn to be believed, the conditional and the following must already have been decided. That is absurd."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4af71537dd2f4440af16d8d194c80c4b","ordinal":356,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.115","text":"\n\nSo the sound conditional is inapprehensible. The leading antecedent is problematic too. For the leading antecedent, as they say, is the antecedent in a conditional of the kind that begins with something true and ends with something true."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ae8a54471981464081392a3cd5702f44","ordinal":357,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.116","text":"\n\nAnd if the sign reveals the consequent, the consequent is either evident or non-evident. If evident, it will not even need anything to reveal it. It will be apprehended together with the sign and will not be signified by it, so the sign will not be a sign of it either. If non-evident, then, since there is undecidable disagreement about non-evident things—which of them are true and which false, and in general whether any of them is true—it will be non-evident whether the conditional ends in something true. And this brings with it the consequence that it is non-evident whether its antecedent is the leading antecedent."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-12a2842b6f174cb29a9c12ebed21ef91","ordinal":358,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.117","text":"\n\nBut let us leave these points aside too. The sign cannot reveal the consequent, given that the thing signified is relative to the sign and for that reason is apprehended together with it. Relatives are apprehended together with one another. The right cannot be apprehended as right of the left before the left, nor the reverse, and the same holds for the other relatives. In the same way it will not be possible for the sign to be apprehended as sign of the thing signified before the thing signified."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cb7f59ed4f4b475e9780d9f98e334570","ordinal":359,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.118","text":"\n\nAnd if the sign is not apprehended before the thing signified, it cannot reveal it either, since that thing is apprehended at the same time as the sign and not after it. So the sign is inconceivable as far as what those of other persuasions say in more general terms goes, too. For they say that it is both relative and revelatory of the thing signified, to which they say it is relative."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-64c098ff69444517951ee2ca4c192c5f","ordinal":360,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 2 · §2.119","text":"\n\nHence if it is relative, and relative to the thing signified, it must certainly be apprehended together with the thing signified, as left is with right, up with down, and so with the other relatives. But if it reveals the thing signified, it must certainly be apprehended before it, so that, being recognized first, it may lead us to a conception of the thing that becomes known from it."}]}